00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.720
Future drives will continue the climb while collecting new data on the layered terrain.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:09.180
The rover also captured a large area of polygon patterns on the surface near mount sharp. These formations stand out because Mars lacks the plate tectonics, vulcanism, and flowing water that reshape Earth's landscapes.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:14.580
The polygons likely record ancient environmental conditions preserved in the regolith.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.580
The absence of resurfacing processes leaves these features intact for study.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:25.080
The images add to the record of how the Martian surface has evolved over billions of years.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:33.260
On a different note, researchers tracked synchronized radio pulses from binaries containing a white dwarf and a smaller m-dwarf companion.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:40.680
The compact dead star and its red dwarf partner orbit in a configuration that generates long period radio emissions.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:47.000
Observations helped clarify why these particular pairs repeat their signals on regular time scales.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.740
Data tie the timing of the bursts directly to the binary orbital period.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:58.380
Follow-up monitoring will examine whether the same emission mechanism appears across wider orbital separations.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:05.900
Johnson Space Center is adapting its astronaut training facilities as private companies develop their own orbital platforms.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.880
Tours show existing simulators already incorporating hardware from multiple commercial providers.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:17.800
The changes prepare crews for operations that will extend beyond the International Space Station era.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:24.000
Officials are mapping how training timelines will evolve once commercial stations host long-duration missions.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.600
The work includes coordination between NASA and industry partners on shared procedures.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:37.440
Images returned by Voyager 2 on August 22, 1989 showed the ice giant possesses a ring system.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:44.460
The discovery came 12 years after launch and marked the final planetary encounter of Voyager 2's primary mission.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.680
Ground teams confirmed the rings through multiple camera angles before announcing the finding.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.460
The data remain a key reference for models of Neptune's faint ring arcs.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:59.660
The announcement followed the August 20, 1977 launch from Cape Canaveral.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:05.020
Those images continue to inform studies of the outer solar system's smallest ring systems.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:14.220
An astronaut on Expedition 72 photographed the 2025 Prayag Maha Kumbamela from orbit using a Nikon Z9.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:19.220
The image shows millions of pilgrims gathered along the Sacred River banks in India.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:26.240
The event occurs once every 144 years and ranks among the largest temporary assemblies on Earth.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:31.740
The orbital perspective reveals the scale of the gathering in a single wide field frame.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:37.220
Astro underscore Pettit posted additional photos from the same mission on social platforms.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:45.130
