00:06:56.730 --> 00:07:01.810
The flight marks the start of a series of missions planned from the New Mexico site.

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:06.690
Data from the instruments will support atmospheric and astrophysics investigations.

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:15.650
In the early 2000s, NASA sent Salmonella bacteria aboard space shuttle missions to study how microgravity alters microbial behavior.

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:21.830
The organisms returned with changes in virulence that researchers had not anticipated before flight.

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:28.110
Following ground studies later identified specific genes linked to the increased pathogenicity.

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:33.210
The findings prompted new protocols for crew health monitoring on long duration flights.

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:39.390
A single composite image records nine hours of star trails centered on the southern celestial pole.

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:44.450
The photographer used a timelapse rig to condense an entire night into one frame.

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:49.790
Earth's rotation produces the circular star path around the pole visible in the shot.

00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:54.750
The image also shows the Milky Way's bright core glowing against the dark sky.

00:07:55.570 --> 00:08:01.890
Rodrigo Eduardo Muñoz captured the scene with an overnight setup that summarized the full rotation in a single view.

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:08.270
White dwarf and red dwarf binaries are turning out to be reliable producers of repeating radio pulses.

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:17.410
The compact stellar remnant and its cooler companion orbit so closely that their interaction generates periodic bursts detectable from Earth.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:24.870
Data now tie the timing of these signals directly to the binary period rather than to isolated flare activity.

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:32.270
Follow-up radio monitoring will test whether the same mechanism operates across a wider range of orbital separations.

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:39.530
The systems offer a natural laboratory for studying magnetized plasma flows under extreme gravitational conditions.

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:48.490
A pair of stars locked in a tight orbit can generate radio emission that behaves like a natural laser repeating with clock-like precision.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:56.030
Picture two objects whose combined mass equals roughly one sun yet fit inside of volume the size of a large city.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:04.230
The white dwarf's intense gravity pulls material from the red dwarf creating a magnetized plasma bridge that rotates with the binary.

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:14.050
Every time the bridge sweeps across our line of sight a narrow beam of coherent radio waves reaches us, sometimes from systems thousands of light years away.

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:24.270
The pulses can last minutes and repeat on time scales of hours to days, a cadence set by the orbital motion rather than by any internal clock inside either star.

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:34.150
One measured system shows the radio beam sweeping past Earth at nearly the speed of light while the stars themselves orbit at only tens of kilometers per second.

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:41.550
Astronomers still cannot explain why the emission stays so narrowly beamed and so stable over years of observation.

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:47.610
The same binaries should produce messy irregular flares yet the signal remains orderly.

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:55.430
The unanswered question is what exact plasma process converts the orbital energy into such a clean repeating radio laser.

